        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The hypothesis upon which this study is based is that the human population is not homogeneous in radiosensitivity, but that radiosensitive sub-groups exist which would suffer an increased incidence of detrimental radiation effects, and distort the shape of the dose response relationship. The radiosensitivity of these groups depends on the expression levels of specific proteins. In a previous proposal funded by NASA the investigators used knock-out mice to show unequivocally that haploinsufficiency for the ATM gene resulted in increased sensitivity to gamma-rays for both ocular cataracts (a deterministic effect) and oncogenic transformation in embryo fibroblasts (a stochastic effect). 
<p>
It would be extraordinarily difficult and expensive to conduct similar experiments with the high energy Fe-59 ions of principal interest to NASA, because of the need to breed a large number of animals, all of the appropriate age, to be available on the few occasions each year when Fe-59 ions are available at the Brookhaven Laboratory. It would be even more difficult and expensive to expand the study to include a number of other genes that are suspected of being involved with radiosensitivity, and to investigate the possible interaction of these various genes. 
<p>
The investigators instead chose to screen cells heterozygous for a number of genes; ATM, BRCA1, BRCA2 and Rad9, as well as double heterozygotes for pairs of these genes. Wild type animals as well as heterozygotes and double heterozygotes were exposed to graded doses of x-rays at Columbia or Fe-56 heavy ions at Brookhaven National Laboratory, and thymocytes assayed for early apoptotic death. By this means they planned to discover which genes or combinations of genes, are important in conferring radiosensitivity. For genes, or combinations of genes that look promising for this apoptotic endpoint, more extensive studies would be carried out with the more relevant endpoint oncogenic transformation using the embryo fibroblasts. This strategy was based on preliminary studies with gamma-rays and cell lines with a mutated gene (namely ATM) which show that a reduction in radiation-induced apoptosis is mirrored by an increase in oncogenic transformation. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for this study isn’t available.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Radiation exposures in Space are relatively difficult to reduce, and impossible to eliminate entirely. At the same time, other risks to humans in the hostile environment in space may be more acute than those of radiation. For this reason, larger annual dose limits have been tolerated for astronauts than recommended by NCRP for radiation workers on the ground, though career limits of risk have been roughly equalized. 
<p>
There are now two clear instances from human epidemiological studies which suggest that the human population is not homogeneous in its response to radiation, but rather that radiosensitive sub-groups exist. One involves the clustering of meningiomas in certain North African families where children were epilated with x-rays in the course of the treatment of Tinea Capitis. The other involves a study of breast cancer in young people monitored by frequent diagnostic x-rays in the management of scoliosis. In this case, the dose-response was significantly greater for women who reported a family history of breast cancer in first or second degree relatives. While in each of these instances there is a clear indication of a radiosensitive sub-group, the gene(s) involved were not identified though there were obvious suspects, ATM in the case of the North African children and BRCA1 in the case of the young people with scoliosis. 
<p>
Against this background, the investigators chose to study the effect on the biological response to heavy particles, similar to those found in space of cells heterozygous for three low frequency, high penetrance genes, namely ATM, BRCA1 and Rad9. This study was possible because knock-out mice, heterozygous for each of these genes, were available. Furthermore, by cross-breeding these animals the investigators were able to obtain animals heterozygous for pairs of these genes. 
<p>
Fibroblasts from mid-term embryos were harvested from all of these animal types, irradiated with heavy ions at Brookhaven, and the incidence of oncogenic transformat ion assessed (this is an in vitro surrogate for carcinogenesis). At the same time, thymocyes were harvested from each animal type, irradiated at Brookhaven, and the incidence of early cell death by apoptosis assayed.. For all three genes, the incidence of radiation induced transformation was greater in heterozygous than in wild type cells, and conversely the incidence of radiation-induced apoptosis was lower in heterozygous cells. The reduced apoptotic levels associated with heterozygous genotypes shows that more cells with unrepaired DNA survive, leading to increased transformation. 
<p>
Mutations and/or polymorphisms are present in the homologues of these genes in the human population, suggesting an explanation for the presence of a radiosensitive sub-population of approximately five percent. The study of the double heterozygotes also raises an interesting point. The cells heterozygous for both the ATM and Rad9 genes were more radiosensitive than cells heterozygous for ATM or Rad9 alone, suggesting an additive effect of the two genes. By contrast, cells heterozygous for both ATM and BRCA1 were no more radiosensitive than cells heterozygous for ATM alone. A possible explanation for this difference is that the ATM and BRCA1 genes operate in the same pathway, whereas ATM and Rad9 do not. 
<p>
